WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.590
The document is open. At the top it says, Q3 Innovation Plan.

00:05.590 --> 00:12.578
The cursor blinks. Your inbox is full of requests, your team wants a direction, and

00:12.578 --> 00:17.702
somebody wants a polished plan by Friday. I know that room.

00:17.702 --> 00:22.360
The pressure makes the blank page feel like the problem.

00:22.360 --> 00:29.348
So you open a tool, ask for ideas, and in thirty seconds you have a

00:29.348 --> 00:34.472
confident looking answer. Now what? Who will decide which idea matters?

00:34.472 --> 00:41.460
What evidence would make that choice responsible? What data did you put into the tool

00:41.460 --> 00:47.981
to get there? A beautiful page can hide those questions for a little while.

00:47.981 --> 00:54.969
It cannot answer them. In the opening of Accelerated, I start with that blinking cursor

00:54.969 --> 01:01.957
because it is familiar. The book begins with product teams carrying noise, conflicting priorities, and

01:01.957 --> 01:08.944
documents that do not move decisions. The work here is to give the team a

01:08.944 --> 01:15.000
way to learn, choose, and keep its judgment when the page fills up.

01:15.000 --> 01:19.972
A product system sounds larger than it needs to be.

01:19.972 --> 01:24.945
Think about the last idea that arrived in a meeting.

01:24.945 --> 01:30.414
Someone noticed a customer problem. Someone else had a competing story.

01:30.414 --> 01:37.873
A third person asked for a number nobody had. The team left with another document

01:37.873 --> 01:45.331
to write. A system gives those moments a path. It helps you record the signal,

01:45.331 --> 01:51.796
name the decision, state what you believe, and come back to what happened.

01:51.796 --> 01:59.254
AI can help organize messy input, test the shape of an argument, and make a

01:59.254 --> 02:05.221
first draft. The judgment still belongs to people who understand the consequences.

02:05.221 --> 02:12.680
That is the argument in the first chapter. I describe AI as a strategy partner,

02:12.680 --> 02:20.138
a signal amplifier, and a structure builder. Those are jobs it may help with, under

02:20.138 --> 02:25.608
the right conditions. They are not permission to trust every output.

02:25.608 --> 02:33.066
If it gives you five polished options, you still need to ask who benefits, what

02:33.066 --> 02:40.525
is missing, and which option you can test safely. Speed can create room to think.

02:40.525 --> 02:45.000
It can also fill that room with more noise.

02:45.000 --> 02:52.459
Look at this map. Signals. Alignment. Momentum. It is a way to navigate the workshop.

02:52.459 --> 02:59.420
First, take the raw material coming at you and find a question worth answering.

02:59.420 --> 03:04.392
Then give the people carrying the decision a shared direction.

03:04.392 --> 03:11.354
Then build a rhythm that lets the next round of evidence change the plan.

03:11.354 --> 03:16.823
The book organizes its printed chapters as Systems, Align, and Momentum.

03:16.823 --> 03:23.785
The workshop uses the three labels on this existing infographic as a visual guide.

03:23.785 --> 03:31.243
You will see where those paths cross; the graphic is not a replacement for the

03:31.243 --> 03:37.210
chapters. In these first lessons we stay close to the signal side.

03:37.210 --> 03:44.669
We will choose one decision, work backward to evidence, and ask what a small test

03:44.669 --> 03:52.127
could teach us. There is a human thread through the book, too: Sam, a product

03:52.127 --> 03:57.597
manager who has talent but keeps getting pulled into reactive work.

03:57.597 --> 04:05.055
That story makes the pressure visible. It is a teaching story from the book, not

04:05.055 --> 04:12.514
a claim about a current customer. Keep that distinction in view as we move between

04:12.514 --> 04:15.000
story, worksheet, and public proof.

04:15.000 --> 04:20.934
Before you try any prompt, decide what information can leave your desk.

04:20.934 --> 04:26.374
The book's data-safety note comes before the chapters for a reason.

04:26.374 --> 04:33.791
Customer details, finances, internal strategy, and company records can carry obligations that a convenient text

04:33.791 --> 04:41.209
box does not erase. My working rule for this course is simple: use public information,

04:41.209 --> 04:47.143
material your organization has approved for that use, or a made-up example.

04:47.143 --> 04:53.571
If you are unsure, stop and check the policy that governs your work.

04:53.571 --> 05:00.989
Do not put restricted material into an external model to make the worksheet feel realistic.

05:00.989 --> 05:08.407
We can learn the method with a synthetic case. Imagine a team trying to improve

05:08.407 --> 05:15.824
the first week for new members. We can invent a harmless request list and test

05:15.824 --> 05:23.242
our reasoning without using anyone's account, message, or history. On the screen, watch the example

05:23.242 --> 05:30.659
move into a three-column boundary: public, approved internal, restricted. “Approved internal” means approved for this

05:30.659 --> 05:36.099
particular tool and purpose, not merely available to you at work.

05:36.099 --> 05:43.516
This boundary follows the decision file through every lesson. It is part of product judgment,

05:43.516 --> 05:45.000
not an appendix.

05:45.000 --> 05:52.461
Let's make the first page of your decision file. It starts with a decision, not

05:52.461 --> 05:59.921
a tool. Write one sentence: “We need to decide whether to...” It could be whether

05:59.921 --> 06:07.382
to change an onboarding step, simplify a service request, or test a different way to

06:07.382 --> 06:14.843
explain an offer. Keep the scope small enough that someone could make a choice after

06:14.843 --> 06:21.806
seeing evidence. Now write who owns that choice and when it must be made.

06:21.806 --> 06:27.277
If nobody owns it, name the role you need to ask.

06:27.277 --> 06:34.738
Underneath, write what would go wrong if you guessed. Then mark the inputs you might

06:34.738 --> 06:39.712
want to use. Public, approved for this purpose, or restricted.

06:39.712 --> 06:44.686
If the real inputs are restricted, make a safe stand-in.

06:44.686 --> 06:51.152
Preserve the shape of the problem; remove the person and the private facts.

06:51.152 --> 06:56.126
You are allowed to pause the video while you write.

06:56.126 --> 07:03.586
When you resume, you should have a cover sheet a colleague could read without asking

07:03.586 --> 07:11.047
what you are trying to decide. You should also know which inputs will stay out

07:11.047 --> 07:18.508
of any AI tool. That is a useful first result, even if the plan itself

07:18.508 --> 07:20.000
is still unfinished.

07:20.000 --> 07:27.812
Take one more look at your sentence. Could a fast draft make that decision for

07:27.812 --> 07:35.625
you? Probably not. It might help you list options, put questions in order, or notice

07:35.625 --> 07:43.438
that an assumption is doing too much work. But a person still has to decide

07:43.438 --> 07:49.167
what matters and what risk the team is willing to carry.

07:49.167 --> 07:54.896
That is why I ask you to keep the decision file.

07:54.896 --> 08:02.708
A chat window can be useful for a moment; the file makes the reasoning visible

08:02.708 --> 08:10.521
when the moment has passed. Write one line under the cover: “What would I need

08:10.521 --> 08:15.729
to learn before I choose?” Do not answer it yet.

08:15.729 --> 08:23.542
Let the question sit. In the next lesson we will bring that question into a

08:23.542 --> 08:31.354
crowded planning room. We will look at three possible paths and choose one worth testing.

08:31.354 --> 08:37.083
If you have only a vague problem now, that is enough.

08:37.083 --> 08:42.812
A good first page does not pretend to know the answer.

08:42.812 --> 08:50.625
It tells the truth about the decision, the evidence you have, and the information you

08:50.625 --> 08:58.438
have permission to use. Keep that first page open. The rest of this workshop will

08:58.438 --> 09:00.000
build on it.
